      "id": 1,
      "text": "Newald was originally called Ross after a pioneer settler before being renamed in 1906 to honor a local landowner.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newald%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early promoters envisioned Newald as a metropolis of the north, complete with a hotel, mill, blacksmith shop, and a saloon.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newald%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A post office was established in Newald in 1905 and remained in operation until it was discontinued in 1985.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newald%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Newald sits at an elevation of one thousand five hundred and seventy feet above sea level in Forest County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://roadsidethoughts.com/wi/newald-xx-forest-profile.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Early construction materials for Newald were shipped by sleigh and team from the south during the winter of 1905.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newald%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Newald was platted in 1905 on a rail line that was under construction between Laona and Sauders.",
